Chapter: RenderCheck
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Safari chapter Geom
  cites Safari chapter World
  cites Safari chapter Render
  cites Safari chapter SafariCritter
  cites Judge chapter Grade
  cites Gold chapter RenderGold

 Grade the Render port's mapping spine against wasm/render.zig, and SafariCritter
 against wasm/safari_critter.zig.

 THREE SEAMS AND THREE STRENGTHS OF ORACLE, which is unusual here and is worth
 stating plainly rather than letting four green rows imply four equal ones.
 render.zig's whole public surface is Chain, Pose, at and frame; buildChain,
 Mapper, mapPt and the ground and rail emitters are private to the file, so what
 can be graded is decided by the zig and not by the port.

   r-at     A REAL ORACLE, and the one that matters. render.at is pub, so the
            probe calls the actual function on the actual world. Every value in
            this stream came out of the game.

   r-prev   THE DEFINITION. mapPt's prev branch is private, but it is exactly
            geom.curToNext followed by geom.toRider and both of those ARE pub, so
            the probe composes the two real functions in that order. What is not
            being tested is that render.zig's private mapPt composes them in that
            order; what IS being tested is every arithmetic step inside it, on the
            real segments, and that the port picks the inverse transform for the
            behind joint rather than the forward one.

   r-chain  THE WEAKEST, and it does not pretend otherwise. buildChain is private
            and `at` never consults the caps -- hand it a chain of any length and
            it composes it -- so the chain's EXTENT is not observable through
            render.zig's public surface at all. The probe walks the real world's
            exit_to and terminates, both graded exactly in WorldCheck, but
            LOOK_AHEAD and MAX_CHAIN are transcribed from render.zig's source.
            Those two integers are the only value in this port that is copied
            rather than measured. They stop being so as soon as the collection
            lands: render.cull_seg is pub and accumulates n_cows + n_pigs over
            chain indices 3 and up, so a frame() call turns the chain's extent
            into a number the world's own graded counts predict. PORTING_NOTES E1
            carries that plan.

   sc-*     A REAL ORACLE. safari_critter.cornerCritters is pub.

 THE CHAIN STILL EARNS ITS ROW. Both of its branches fire across the nineteen
 starts: the look-ahead caps the first thirteen at seven, and the terminus break
 walks the tail down six, five, four, three, two, one. A chain that ran past the
 finish line, or looped at it, or dropped the terminating segment for being one
 index past the cap, is caught -- and the off-by-one at the cap test is the whole
 reason the port reads ch.len at the TOP of the loop.

 THE SAMPLE POINTS ARE THE ONES RENDER AUTHORS AT, not a grid for its own sake.
 The four `a` are the segment start, its middle, its end edge -- where every joint
 quad and every corner rail begins -- and the 160 m beyond where the intersection
 tower stands. The four `x` are the left edge, the centre line, the right edge and
 the tower's 20 m off it. So the composition is measured where the module will
 actually ask for it, including well past the end of a segment, which is where a
 wrong join rotation shows up largest.

 THE TWO POSES DIFFER IN EVERY FIELD and one of them looks LEFT. A yaw near zero
 makes the rider transform nearly the identity in `right` and hides a sign error
 in the sine; -0.3 rad does not.

 The chains are the three shapes the route produces: capped at the look-ahead,
 running into the terminus, and the short tail. The four previous segments turn
 both ways, including seg 12's right turn into the pond corner.

 We say:

Section: The World

  segs : List Segment
  segs = build-world

  n-segs : Integer
  n-segs = list-length segs

Section: The Chain Streams

  chain-flat : Integer -> List Integer
  chain-flat (i) =
    if i >= n-segs then []
    else (build-chain segs i) & chain-flat (i + 1)

  chain-lens : Integer -> List Integer
  chain-lens (i) =
    if i >= n-segs then []
    else [list-length (build-chain segs i)] & chain-lens (i + 1)

Section: The Sample Points

 Where render authors: the segment start, middle, end edge, and the tower's
 distance beyond it; the left edge, centre line, right edge, and the tower's
 offset off it. These two are render.zig's tower placement constants, and they are
 here rather than in the port because the port has not reached the towers yet --
 they choose sample points, so they are test inputs and not expected values.

  tower-beyond : Real
  tower-beyond = 160.0

  tower-right : Real
  tower-right = 20.0

  sample-as : Segment -> List Real
  sample-as (s) = [0.0, s.length / 2.0, s.length, s.length + tower-beyond]

  sample-xs : Segment -> List Real
  sample-xs (s) = [0.0, s.width / 2.0, s.width, s.width / 2.0 + tower-right]

  poses : List Pose
  poses = [Pose { along = 120.0, across = 0.6, yaw = 0.05, hw = 2.0 }, Pose { along = 480.0, across = 0.0 - 1.2, yaw = 0.0 - 0.3, hw = 2.0 }]

  sample-starts : List Integer
  sample-starts = [0, 12, 16]

  prev-indices : List Integer
  prev-indices = [0, 1, 12, 17]

Section: The at Stream

 Pose outermost, then chain, then depth, then `a`, then `x` -- the probe's own
 nesting. Right before forward at every point, as RiderPt is written.

  at-over-xs : List Integer, Pose, Integer, List Real, Real, Integer -> List Real
  at-over-xs (ch) (pose) (d) (xs) (a) (j) =
    if j >= list-length xs then []
    else let p = at segs ch pose d a (list-at xs j)
    in [p.right, p.forward] & at-over-xs ch pose d xs a (j + 1)

  at-over-as : List Integer, Pose, Integer, List Real, List Real, Integer -> List Real
  at-over-as (ch) (pose) (d) (as) (xs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length as then []
    else (at-over-xs ch pose d xs (list-at as i) 0) & at-over-as ch pose d as xs (i + 1)

  at-over-ds : List Integer, Pose, Integer -> List Real
  at-over-ds (ch) (pose) (d) =
    if d >= list-length ch then []
    else let sg = list-at segs (list-at ch d)
    in (at-over-as ch pose d (sample-as sg) (sample-xs sg) 0) & at-over-ds ch pose (d + 1)

  at-over-chains : Pose, Integer -> List Real
  at-over-chains (pose) (i) =
    if i >= list-length sample-starts then []
    else (at-over-ds (build-chain segs (list-at sample-starts i)) pose 0) & at-over-chains pose (i + 1)

  at-stream : Integer -> List Real
  at-stream (i) =
    if i >= list-length poses then []
    else (at-over-chains (list-at poses i) 0) & at-stream (i + 1)

Section: The Behind-Joint Stream

 Segment outermost, then pose -- the probe nests these the other way round from
 the at stream, and the order is the assertion, so the port follows it.

 THE CHAIN HANDED IN IS EMPTY, and that is the point rather than a shortcut: the
 prev mapper does not consult it. The previous segment is not on the chain at all
 -- the chain begins at the rider's current segment and only runs forward -- which
 is precisely why this branch exists. Passing an empty list proves the port reads
 nothing it should not.

  prev-over-xs : Segment, Pose, List Real, Real, Integer -> List Real
  prev-over-xs (pv) (pose) (xs) (a) (j) =
    if j >= list-length xs then []
    else let p = map-pt segs [] pose (prev-map pv) a (list-at xs j)
    in [p.right, p.forward] & prev-over-xs pv pose xs a (j + 1)

  prev-over-as : Segment, Pose, List Real, List Real, Integer -> List Real
  prev-over-as (pv) (pose) (as) (xs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length as then []
    else (prev-over-xs pv pose xs (list-at as i) 0) & prev-over-as pv pose as xs (i + 1)

  prev-over-poses : Segment, Integer -> List Real
  prev-over-poses (pv) (i) =
    if i >= list-length poses then []
    else (prev-over-as pv (list-at poses i) (sample-as pv) (sample-xs pv) 0) & prev-over-poses pv (i + 1)

  prev-stream : Integer -> List Real
  prev-stream (i) =
    if i >= list-length prev-indices then []
    else (prev-over-poses (list-at segs (list-at prev-indices i)) 0) & prev-stream (i + 1)

Section: The Corner Pair Streams

 Every creature against both turn directions. The two that yield nothing -- the
 terminus and the duck pond -- are first and last, so a species table that fell
 through to a default would be caught at both ends.

  creatures : List Creature
  creatures = [NoCreature, Elephant, Giraffe, Zebra, Rhino, DuckPond]

  turns : List Boolean
  turns = [True, False]

  sc-pair : Creature, Integer -> List Critter
  sc-pair (c) (j) =
    if j >= list-length turns then []
    else (corner-critters c 300.0 (list-at turns j) 2.0) & sc-pair c (j + 1)

  sc-all : Integer -> List Critter
  sc-all (i) =
    if i >= list-length creatures then []
    else (sc-pair (list-at creatures i) 0) & sc-all (i + 1)

  sc-count-pair : Creature, Integer -> List Integer
  sc-count-pair (c) (j) =
    if j >= list-length turns then []
    else [list-length (corner-critters c 300.0 (list-at turns j) 2.0)] & sc-count-pair c (j + 1)

  sc-counts : Integer -> List Integer
  sc-counts (i) =
    if i >= list-length creatures then []
    else (sc-count-pair (list-at creatures i) 0) & sc-counts (i + 1)

  cr-cps : List Critter, Integer -> List Integer
  cr-cps (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [(list-at cs i).codepoint] & cr-cps cs (i + 1)

  cr-faces : List Critter, Integer -> List Boolean
  cr-faces (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else [(list-at cs i).face-right] & cr-faces cs (i + 1)

  cr-place : List Critter, Integer -> List Real
  cr-place (cs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length cs then []
    else let c = list-at cs i
    in [c.along, c.across, c.height] & cr-place cs (i + 1)

Section: Entry

 Chain indices, lengths, codepoints and counts take NO tolerance: they are
 structure. The critter placement is arithmetic over authored constants and takes
 the ordinary relative gate.

 THE TWO MAPPED-POINT SEAMS TAKE A MIXED GATE, AND THEY ARE THE FIRST SEAM IN
 METRES TO NEED ONE. Every other world-coordinate stream in this port holds at a
 plain 1e-6 relative. These do not, and the reason is cancellation again -- the
 third distinct place it has driven a gate here, after the tree's horizon and the
 sun's disc, and the first where the amplifier is the CHAIN rather than the
 screen.

 The worst case was measured, not guessed. At the rider's second chain segment, a
 point 160 m past its end composes down through two joins to a bottom-left frame
 coordinate of 1235.7 m -- and lands 1.52 m to the rider's left. `right` is
 -1115.7 * sin(yaw) + 54.3 * cos(yaw): two terms near 55 m that very nearly
 cancel, computed from a coordinate over a kilometre out. The amplification is
 815x, and the absolute error is 2.9e-5 m -- twenty-nine microns on scenery a
 kilometre away, which is nothing, and 1.9e-5 relative, which is twenty times the
 gate every other metre seam here passes.

 SO THE ERROR IS NOT PROPORTIONAL TO THE ANSWER. It is proportional to the largest
 coordinate the composition passes through, because that is what f32 has to hold
 on the way. That makes the floor a REPRESENTATION granularity rather than a
 fudge, and it is set as one:

   r-at    reaches 2015 m, where one f32 ulp is 1.22e-4 m. Floor 2.5e-4 -- two
           ulp at that range. Measured requirement 1.05e-4; the check reddens at
           1e-4 and passes at 1.2e-4, so the floor carries about 2x.
   r-prev  the behind joint composes ONE join, not six, and reaches 660 m, where
           one f32 ulp is 6.1e-5 m. Floor 6e-5 -- one ulp at that range. It
           reddens at 1e-5 and passes at 2e-5, so it carries about 3x.

 THE TWO FLOORS DIFFER BECAUSE THE TWO REACHES DIFFER, which is D5's rule applied
 rather than restated: the SHAPE of the gate comes from the failure mode and both
 seams share it, but the NUMBER comes from what each seam actually has to admit,
 and giving the behind joint the chain's floor would have been four times slacker
 than anything it needs. The relative term stays at 1e-6 in both, where the rest
 of the port has it, and it is what governs the far coordinates the floor is far
 too small for.

 Neither floor is fitted to the measurement. Each is the f32 granularity at its
 own seam's working range, which is the number that would still be right if the
 samples moved.

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "r-chainlen" (chain-lens 0) g-r-chainlen)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "r-chain   " (chain-flat 0) g-r-chain)
    print-line-uni (grade-px   "r-at      " (at-stream 0) g-r-at 0.00025 0.000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-px   "r-prev    " (prev-stream 0) g-r-prev 0.00006 0.000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "sc-n      " (sc-counts 0) g-sc-n)
    print-line-uni (grade-ints "sc-cp     " (cr-cps (sc-all 0) 0) g-sc-cp)
    print-line-uni (grade-bools "sc-face   " (cr-faces (sc-all 0) 0) g-sc-face)
    print-line-uni (grade-rel  "sc-place  " (cr-place (sc-all 0) 0) g-sc-place 0.000001)
  end
